{"meta":{"chapter_title":"AI Ethics and Ethobot 3.2","title":"Module 4 Ethics Studio","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting module 4 ethics studio to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"04","section":"08","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_ethics_and_ethobot_3_2","cat531_supplement_module_4_ethics_studio","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/04/07"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_4_8_module_4_ethics_studio_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_4_8_module_4_ethics_studio_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_4_8_module_4_ethics_studio_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_4_8_module_4_ethics_studio_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":8,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Module 4 Ethics Studio\n\n![Module 4 Ethics Studio textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/04-08-module-4-ethics-studio.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nThe module taught the ethics tools one at a time: read a dialogue for the reasoning shift, weigh a value conflict on a tension map, find the silent stakeholder, write a consent clause that overrides the vendor default. Real adoption decisions do not arrive sorted into those boxes. A single tool drops all four problems on your desk at once, and the studio is where you prove you can run them together on one realistic case without losing any of them. Integration is its own competency; a teacher who can ace each tool in isolation can still produce an incoherent decision when the privacy answer and the equity answer pull in different directions.\n\nThe studio also exposes the trap that separates a checklist from judgment. It is tempting to run each tool, get four clean sub-answers, and staple them into a decision. But the answers interact: the silent stakeholder you found in the stakeholder pass is often the same student the consent rule and the negotiated decision must protect, and a strong capstone shows those threads converging on one coherent call, not four parallel paragraphs that happen to share a cover page.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Sequence the module's tools (purpose, stakeholder check, privacy and consent check, negotiated tension map) into one integrated decision.\n- Carry a finding from one tool into the next, so the silent stakeholder shapes both the consent rule and the final decision.\n- Keep the learning purpose primary, so efficiency and convenience never silently override why the tool was considered.\n- Record a rejected suggestion and the evidence behind the whole judgment, so a mentor teacher can audit the integration.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA school proposes an AI reading-level assessment that records each student reading aloud and scores fluency automatically. Run only one tool and you get a partial, possibly dangerous answer: the efficiency case alone says \"adopt it, it saves screening time.\" The studio asks for the integrated judgment. State the purpose (identify students who need support, not rank them), run the stakeholder check (the silent stakeholder is the student whose home language is not English, whose accent the model may misread), run the consent check (voice is biometric, so opt-in and deletion are required), and only then build the negotiated decision. The single-tool answer and the integrated answer point in different directions, which is exactly why integration is the skill being assessed.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nThe ethics studio is an integration method: a fixed sequence in which each tool's output feeds the next, ending in one defensible decision rather than four separate verdicts. The sequence matters because the tools are not independent.\n\nStart with purpose, because it governs everything after it. If the purpose is \"identify students who need support,\" any feature that ranks or labels students is already in tension with the goal, and you will read the rest of the case through that frame.\n\nRun the stakeholder check next, and carry its output forward. The silent stakeholder you surface (the non-native English speaker the fluency model may penalize) is not a finding you file away; it is the person the consent rule and the negotiated decision must now protect. An integrated studio threads that student through every later step.\n\nRun the privacy and consent check on the specific data type. Voice is biometric, which raises the bar: parental opt-in and a deletion rule are not optional add-ons but conditions of adoption. Notice this finding compounds with the stakeholder finding, since the most-affected student is also the one whose biometric data is most consequential.\n\n**Recent evidence.** \"Voice is biometric\" is a legal category, not a course slogan. The FTC's 2025 amended COPPA Rule (effective June 23, 2025; compliance by April 22, 2026) expands children's \"personal information\" to include biometric identifiers and sets specific notice, use, and deletion terms for collecting a child's voice recording; the only no-consent path requires that no other data is collected, the audio serves only the child's request, and the file is deleted immediately. Illinois BIPA likewise names a voiceprint as a biometric identifier and requires written notice, written consent, and a published retention-and-destruction schedule.\n\nBuild the negotiated decision last, because it must reconcile all the prior outputs at once: the purpose, the silent stakeholder, the biometric constraint, and the real efficiency benefit. The decision keeps a teacher confirmation step on every flag, requires opt-in and short retention, and is defended with evidence (the vendor's fluency model was trained on a single national accent).\n\nThe course anchors operate together here, which is the point. **Teacher professional judgment** sequences the tools and reconciles their outputs into one call. **Critical edtech studies** keeps the silent stakeholder visible from the stakeholder pass through the final decision. **[AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)** raises the consent bar once the data is identified as biometric voice.\n\n## Worked Example: A Capstone Tension Map That Integrates the Module\n\nThe studio asks you to combine the module's tools into one defensible decision on a single realistic case. Below, a teacher integrates stakeholders (04.03), privacy and consent (04.04), and the negotiated-decision map (04.02) for one tool. Work it as numbered steps.\n\nThe capstone case: adopting an AI reading-level assessment that records each student's oral reading.\n\nStep-by-step integrated decision:\n\n- Step 1. State the learning purpose first. The tool should identify students who need reading support, not rank or label them.\n- Step 2. Run the stakeholder check (from 04.03). Silent stakeholder = students whose home language is not English, whose accent the model may misread as low fluency.\n- Step 3. Run the privacy and consent check (from 04.04). Voice recordings of minors require parental opt-in and a deletion rule; voice is biometric.\n- Step 4. Build the negotiated decision map (from 04.02). Pole A = faster, consistent screening. Pole B = accent bias and biometric voice data. Negotiated decision: pilot with teacher confirmation of any low-fluency flag, parental opt-in, and 30-day deletion.\n\nBefore (weak studio submission): \"The AI reading tool is efficient and I made the map clear.\" This skips purpose, the silent stakeholder, and consent.\n\nAfter (strong): \"Integrated decision: purpose is support not ranking; silent stakeholder is non-native-English speakers at risk of accent misreading; voice is biometric so parental opt-in and 30-day deletion are required; negotiated decision keeps a teacher confirmation step on every low-fluency flag. Evidence the accent risk is real = the vendor's fluency model was trained on a single national accent. I rejected the suggestion to auto-place students by score, because that removes the teacher judgment the purpose requires.\"\n\nThe strong submission integrates the module's three tools, cites evidence, and records a rejected suggestion, so a mentor teacher can audit the whole judgment.\n\n**Recent evidence.** The equity legs of this case are documented, not stipulated. Automated speech recognition errors fall disproportionately on African American users, who report feeling \"othered\" by the technology (Mengesha et al., 2021), grounding the accent-misreading risk in a voice-fluency tool. Automated essay ratings show potential bias across writers' race, ethnicity, and language background, including for English language learners (Yamashita, 2025). And the weapons-scanner scenario has a real anchor: in November 2024 the FTC took action against Evolv Technologies for deceptive accuracy claims made to K-12 schools, with documented failures and high false-positive rates, including one school whose raised sensitivity produced roughly a 50 percent false-alarm rate.\n\nThe sequence is not arbitrary: each tool's output feeds the next. Put the integration steps in order.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"State the learning purpose first, because it constrains every later step\", \"Run the stakeholder check and carry the silent stakeholder forward\", \"Run the privacy and consent check on the specific data type\", \"Build the negotiated decision that reconciles purpose, stakeholder, privacy, and benefit\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nA studio submission is integrated, not stapled, when:\n\n1. **Purpose leads:** The stated learning purpose visibly constrains every later step.\n2. **Threads carry:** The silent stakeholder from the stakeholder pass reappears in the consent rule and the final decision.\n3. **Constraints compound:** The biometric or privacy finding raises the bar on the decision, not just on a side note.\n4. **One coherent call:** The negotiated decision reconciles purpose, stakeholder, privacy, and benefit, and names a rejected suggestion.\n\nIf your four sub-answers never reference each other, you have a checklist; integration is when they converge.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A studio submission has four tidy paragraphs: purpose, stakeholders, consent, decision. The consent paragraph never mentions the non-native English speaker the stakeholder paragraph identified. What does this reveal?\" explanation=\"The submission is stapled, not integrated. In a real integration the silent stakeholder threads forward: the same student the stakeholder pass surfaced is the one the consent rule and the final decision must protect. Four parallel paragraphs that never reference each other are a checklist, not a judgment.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It is integrated; all four tools are present\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It is stapled: the threads never carry from one tool to the next\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It needs a fifth tool to connect them\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The paragraphs should be in a different order\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nThe transfer case is the real test: a safety tool where the poles are genuinely close and the purpose pulls toward adoption. Run the integration when there is no easy answer.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"The weapons-detection scanner: integration under conflict\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"A district proposes an AI weapons-detection scanner that images every student entering. The stated purpose is student safety, and the privacy cost is severe. Where do you start the integration?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"With efficiency: it scans faster than a guard\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"Efficiency is the easiest pole to measure and the wrong place to start; the purpose must lead, or the most-measurable pole quietly decides.\"},{\"label\":\"With the stated purpose, because it governs everything after it\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Purpose leads. Here the purpose is safety, which genuinely pulls toward adoption, so the later passes must do real work.\"},{\"label\":\"With the negotiated decision, then justify it\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"Deciding first and justifying after is the checklist trap; the decision must come last so it can reconcile the earlier passes.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"Reconsider: what happens when the most-measurable pole leads?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"It overrules the purpose and the equity findings\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Start with purpose, not the easiest number.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"Run the stakeholder check. Who is the silent stakeholder for a body scanner, and carry them forward.\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Students flagged by a model with higher false-positive rates for certain body types or wheelchairs, who face repeated public searches\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"This is the silent stakeholder. They must reappear in the privacy and the final decision, not be filed away.\"},{\"label\":\"There is no silent stakeholder; everyone is scanned equally\",\"next\":\"n6\",\"feedback\":\"Equal scanning is not equal impact: false positives concentrate on specific groups who absorb repeated public searches.\"}]},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"Re-sequence: which step must come last?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The negotiated decision, so it reconciles the prior passes\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Purpose first, decision last.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"n5\":{\"prompt\":\"Privacy and consent pass: full-body imaging of minors is severe and biometric-adjacent. The purpose (safety) cannot lean on ’support not ranking’ to resolve the tension. What does the negotiated decision look like when the poles are this close?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Adopt fully; safety always outweighs privacy\",\"next\":\"n7\",\"feedback\":\"This lets the purpose override every other pass, which is the integration failing under pressure.\"},{\"label\":\"A conditioned, narrow decision: human review of every flag, no image retention, an opt-out alternative entry, and a re-entry condition on documented false-positive parity\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"This is integration holding under conflict: the silent stakeholder is protected by human review and parity conditions, privacy is protected by no retention, and safety is served without the most-measurable pole overruling the rest.\"},{\"label\":\"Reject outright; any imaging of minors is unacceptable\",\"next\":\"n8\",\"feedback\":\"A flat rejection ignores the genuine safety purpose and offers no re-entry condition; the poles here are too close for a one-pole verdict.\"}]},\"n6\":{\"prompt\":\"Re-examine: does equal scanning mean equal impact?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"No; false positives concentrate on specific groups\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Name them as the silent stakeholder.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"n7\":{\"prompt\":\"Re-anchor: what does letting safety override every pass cost?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The privacy and equity passes become decoration\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"That is why the decision must reconcile the poles, not let one win.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"n8\":{\"prompt\":\"Add what a flat rejection lacks.\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"A conditioned decision with a measurable re-entry condition\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Reconcile the poles instead of picking one.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"end\":{\"prompt\":\"You produced one coherent decision that reconciles purpose, the silent stakeholder, privacy, and benefit.\",\"feedback\":\"Integration holds when the poles are close: purpose leads, the silent stakeholder threads through, privacy compounds, and the decision reconciles all four rather than letting the most-measurable or the stated-purpose pole win alone.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is treating the studio as four separate assignments collated under one heading, so the privacy paragraph never mentions the student the stakeholder paragraph just identified. The second is letting the efficiency benefit, which is the easiest pole to measure, quietly become the deciding factor after the purpose said otherwise. A studio that ends with \"it saves time, so adopt\" has let the most measurable pole overrule the purpose it opened with.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nRun the full sequence on a fresh capstone case: an AI essay-scoring tool a department wants for state-test practice. Purpose: give students fast formative feedback so they revise, not to assign a grade of record. Stakeholder check: the silent stakeholder is the student who writes in a dialect or developmental syntax the model scores as error, the same exposure pattern as the reading-fluency case. Privacy and consent check: essays are student records, so retention must be bounded and vendor training excluded; the bar is high but not biometric, which lets the consent rule be lighter than the voice case demanded. Negotiated decision: adopt for formative use only, with a teacher override on any score that would lower a grade, opt-in for any data leaving the building, and a rejected suggestion (auto-entering the AI score into the gradebook) named with its reason (it converts formative feedback into a high-stakes judgment the purpose forbids). The threads carry, the constraints compound, and the decision reconciles them, which is what distinguishes the studio from running four tools side by side.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nKeep a single-thread note that follows one student through every tool: who the silent stakeholder is, how the consent rule protects them, and how the final decision keeps them safe. A strong reader of a studio submission can trace that thread end to end and can state where the purpose constrained a tempting efficiency shortcut. If your reading treats the four passes as unrelated, the integration the studio assesses is not there.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong studio artifact leads with purpose, threads the silent stakeholder through consent and the final decision, compounds the privacy constraint into the call, and names a rejected suggestion with evidence. Visual neatness is the last layer. If the sub-answers do not reference each other, mark the gap and ask BigAL to test whether your final decision actually protects the stakeholder you identified rather than for a general explanation of the module.\n\nA useful closing line for the artifact sounds like this: \"Because the purpose was support not ranking and the silent stakeholder was the non-native English speaker the fluency model penalizes, the decision keeps a teacher confirmation on every flag and requires opt-in with 30-day deletion; I rejected auto-placement by score because it would override the very judgment the purpose protects.\"\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Explain the difference between a 'stapled' studio submission and an 'integrated' one, using the silent stakeholder as the example of what should thread through.\" answer=\"A stapled submission runs each tool, gets four clean sub-answers, and collates them under one heading, so the privacy paragraph never mentions the student the stakeholder paragraph just identified and the final decision does not visibly follow from the purpose. An integrated submission carries each finding forward: the silent stakeholder surfaced in the stakeholder pass (say, the non-native English speaker the fluency model penalizes) is the same person the consent rule must protect and the same person the negotiated decision keeps safe with a teacher confirmation step. The threads converge on one coherent call. The test is whether the four passes reference each other; if they never do, the integration the studio assesses is absent.\"></self-explain>\n\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the integration method to a case where the tools conflict more sharply: a safety tool (an AI weapons-detection scanner) where the privacy cost is severe but the stated purpose is student safety, not convenience. Here the purpose pulls toward adoption while the privacy and equity passes pull hard against it, and the negotiated decision cannot lean on \"support not ranking\" to resolve the tension. Working this version tests whether your integration method holds when the poles are genuinely close, or whether it only worked because the earlier cases had an obvious answer once the tools were run.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/04-08-module-4-ethics-studio-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"04.08\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 04.08. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Concept Map\n\nThese are the concepts this section connects. Trace how integrating the module's tools threads one stakeholder through evidence-based revision into a coherent decision.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_ai_ethics_and_ethobot_3_2\", \"cat531_supplement_module_4_ethics_studio\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_module_4_ethics_studio\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision captures instructional purpose, equity or privacy boundary, support request, revision rationale, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- Mishra, P., & Koehler, M. J. (2006). [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- Williamson, B., et al. (2023). Re-examining AI, automation and datafication in education. *Learning, Media and Technology, 48*(1). https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2023.2167830\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n- Ning, Y., et al. (2024). Teachers' AI-TPACK: Exploring the relationship between knowledge elements. *Sustainability, 16*(3), 978. https://doi.org/10.3390/su16030978\n- Federal Trade Commission (2025). Children's Online Privacy Protection Rule, Final Rule. *Federal Register*, 90 FR 16164. https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/04/22/2025-05904/childrens-online-privacy-protection-rule\n- Illinois Biometric Information Privacy Act, 740 ILCS 14 (BIPA). https://law.justia.com/codes/illinois/chapter-740/act-740-ilcs-14/\n- Mengesha, Z., et al. (2021). \"I don't think these devices are very culturally sensitive.\" Impact of automated speech recognition errors on African Americans. *Frontiers in Artificial Intelligence, 4*. https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2021.725911\n- Yamashita, T. (2025). Exploring potential biases in GPT-4o's ratings of English language learners' essays. *Language Testing*. https://doi.org/10.1177/02655322251329435\n- Federal Trade Commission (2024). FTC takes action against Evolv Technologies for deceiving users about its AI-powered security screening systems. https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2024/11/ftc-takes-action-against-evolv-technologies-deceiving-users-about-its-ai-powered-security-screening\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_8_module_4_ethics_studio_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_module_4_ethics_studio","stem":"A capstone studio submission justifies an AI reading-level tool by saying 'it is fast and consistent.' What is the missing first move?","options":["A more colorful tension map.","Stating the learning purpose the tool serves, such as identifying students who need reading support rather than ranking them.","The price of the tool.","The vendor's contact information."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Efficiency is only defensible once the tool clearly serves a stated learning purpose.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_8_efficiency_over_purpose","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_module_4_ethics_studio","evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_8_module_4_ethics_studio_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_module_4_ethics_studio","stem":"A capstone map for a voice-recording reading tool evaluates only accuracy. Which module tools does it still need to integrate?","options":["Only a spelling check.","The stakeholder check and the privacy and consent check, since voice is biometric data from minors and accent may be misread.","Only a longer reflection.","Only a citation to the textbook."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A capstone decision integrates stakeholders, privacy and consent, and the negotiated map, not one lens alone.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_8_single_lens_decision","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_module_4_ethics_studio","evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_8_module_4_ethics_studio_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_module_4_ethics_studio","stem":"For an AI tool that records minors' oral reading, which consent and data element must the capstone decision include?","options":["A note that the recordings sound clear.","Parental opt-in and a deletion rule, because voice recordings of minors are biometric data.","The brand of microphone used.","A request that students speak louder."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Voice is biometric as a matter of statute, not just prudence: the FTC's 2025 amended COPPA Rule counts biometric identifiers as children's personal information and sets deletion terms for voice recordings, and Illinois BIPA names a voiceprint explicitly. Recording minors therefore requires parental opt-in and a retention or deletion rule.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_8_single_lens_decision","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_module_4_ethics_studio","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_8_module_4_ethics_studio_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"A capstone decision concludes 'pilot with a teacher confirmation step on every low-fluency flag.' What makes this auditable as professional judgment rather than preference?","options":["It is the shortest possible conclusion.","It cites evidence (the fluency model trained on one accent) and records a rejected alternative (auto-placing students by score) with a reason.","It uses the word pilot, which sounds cautious.","It was reached quickly during class."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A defensible capstone judgment cites evidence and shows the alternative it ruled out and why.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_8_no_rejected_option","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","cat531_supplement_module_4_ethics_studio"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_8_efficiency_over_purpose","pattern":"efficiency_justifies_adoption","description":"Justifying an AI tool by its efficiency while skipping the learning purpose it is supposed to serve","trigger":"learner adopts a tool because it is fast or consistent without stating what learning goal it advances","feedback":"State the learning purpose first. Efficiency is only defensible once the tool clearly serves a specific learning goal.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_8_single_lens_decision","pattern":"deciding_with_one_ethics_lens","description":"Reaching a decision using only one lens (only equity, or only privacy) instead of integrating the module's tools","trigger":"learner's capstone map applies one check and ignores stakeholders, consent, or the negotiated decision","feedback":"Integrate the lenses. A capstone decision should run the stakeholder, privacy and consent, and negotiated-decision checks together.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_8_no_rejected_option","pattern":"decision_without_a_rejected_alternative","description":"Presenting a final decision with no record of an alternative that was considered and rejected for a reason","trigger":"learner states a conclusion but never shows what they ruled out","feedback":"Record at least one rejected option and why. A defensible judgment shows the alternative it turned down.","rail_action":"explain"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"8bfe0c55d261bf329e43a7ece6e096a62cf236ceceb5c4aa3b18163f96f92122","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}